From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maago <maago@example.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 23:39:42 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Posts have authors
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Posts get authors. Writing needs a sign-in, and only the author or
staff publish, archive and delete.

- Post.AuthorID *int64 `maago:"fk=auth.User"`. A model of another app
  is app.Model. A pointer, for the posts from before there were
  accounts: they have no author. Were it int64, not *int64,
  makemigrations would warn

      -- ⚠ Column "author_id" … is NOT NULL without a default, so this fails if "board_post" has rows …

  and maago migrate would fail on a database with posts
- maago makemigrations --name post_author adds the column, its index
  and the foreign key (0004)
- maago gen stops at recent_posts.sql again (as with Hidden in 0015):
  :many Post must select every column of Post. Add author_id
- A view that needs a sign-in takes the user as auth.User, not a
  pointer. That alone makes its routes need one; there is no route
  option. Every view that writes takes it, and so do the forms' GETs:

      func NewPost(ctx context.Context, u auth.User, db maago.DB, in NewPostIn) (PostFormPage, error)
      func CreatePost(ctx context.Context, u auth.User, tx maago.Tx, in NewPostIn) (maago.Result[PostFormPage], error)

  Nobody signed in: a GET goes to the sign-in page (with next, to come
  back), a POST is a 401 (a form cannot be sent again, so it does not
  follow), and an API route is a 401 with WWW-Authenticate: Bearer. The
  comment form is on the post page, so a comment without a sign-in gets
  a 401. u is the door: a view that never reads it still needs it, and
  without it the route is open to anybody. There is no route option for
  it: maago gen answers a maago.LoginRequired with what to write instead
- A new post's author is that user: savePost(ctx, tx, u, …) gives
  InsertPost AuthorID: &u.ID. A frontend that writes through the API
  sends the user's API token as Authorization: Bearer
- mayChange(u auth.User, p Post) error: one line at the top of
  publish, archive and delete. Neither the author nor staff: a 403. An
  old post with no author is staff's. -createsuperuser makes staff

The table is where nobody and the others part. Its roles grow to four:
nobody, bob (another user), ann (the author) and staff, signed in with
auth.SignIn as auth.CreateSuperuser made it. Its diff is the step:

- The forms' GETs, board_new and post_new: nobody 303 (to sign in), the
  others 200. Every write: nobody 401. api_post_create too: the table's
  requests carry the session cookie, which the API takes as a page
  does, so no API token is needed to test it
- A write anybody signed in may do has a row for each of bob, ann and
  staff, each with a board or post of its own
- Publish, archive and delete: bob 403, ann 303 on her post. Staff's
  303 is on a post from before there were accounts, which ann may not
  touch (403): the post with no author is staff's

TestAuthors keeps what the table cannot say: a new post's author is the
user who wrote it.

